<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/house-fire.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/house-fire-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/house-fire.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Fire and Smoke Damage Appraisal in Wisconsin</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">A fire may burn one room, but smoke travels everywhere air does. Fire claims are some of the largest and most emotionally draining a property owner faces, and they leave plenty of room for estimates to differ.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Covers</small><b>Fire, smoke, soot, odor</b></div><div><small>Big gaps</small><b>Clean vs. replace</b></div><div><small>Also includes</small><b>Contents, living expenses</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Fire and smoke damage appraisal in Wisconsin helps owners whose insurer has accepted a fire loss but offered a figure that doesn’t match the real cost of restoring the property. We serve as your appraiser, inspecting the damage ourselves and pricing the full scope, from demolition to final paint.</p>
<h2>Where fire claims split apart</h2>
<p>Most disputes fall into a handful of categories. Knowing them helps you read the carrier’s estimate with a sharper eye.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cleaning versus replacement.</strong> Insurers often propose cleaning and sealing surfaces that may need to be replaced to remove odor and residue.</li>
<li><strong>Extent of smoke migration.</strong> Smoke moves through ductwork, wall cavities and attics. Rooms that look clean can still carry soot and odor.</li>
<li><strong>Structural char.</strong> Whether charred framing can be sealed or must be removed and replaced.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC systems.</strong> Duct cleaning versus replacement, and whether equipment was damaged by heat or corrosive residue.</li>
<li><strong>Code upgrades.</strong> Rebuilding a significantly damaged older home often triggers current electrical, egress and energy code requirements.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Smoke and soot are not all alike</h2>
<p>Burning wood, plastics, protein from a kitchen fire and synthetic furnishings each leave different residues. Some wipe off. Others are oily, acidic and bond to surfaces. The right cleaning or replacement method depends on what burned, so documentation matters. Where the extent of contamination is disputed, independent testing can give the panel something objective to weigh.</p>
<h2>Older homes and fire</h2>
<p>Many Wisconsin homes still have original plaster walls, old-growth trim and hardwood floors. A template estimate that swaps everything for drywall and builder-grade trim may not reflect like kind and quality. We document what was there and price materials and labor to match it as closely as the policy requires.</p>
<h2>Contents and living expenses</h2>
<p>Fire claims usually include personal property and additional living expense alongside the building. Contents inventories are tedious but important; items left off the list can’t be paid. Living expense disputes often involve how long repairs should reasonably take, which ties directly to the building scope.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">If arson or fraud is raised</b>If an insurer questions how a fire started or raises concerns about misrepresentation, stop and talk with a Wisconsin attorney. Those issues are not something an appraisal panel resolves.</div>
<h2>How we work a fire appraisal</h2>
<ol>
<li>Walk the entire structure, including rooms far from the origin, and document soot and odor.</li>
<li>Review the mitigation company’s scope, the carrier’s estimate and any test results.</li>
<li>Bring in specialists where needed, such as an industrial hygienist or structural engineer.</li>
<li>Build a room-by-room estimate covering demolition, cleaning, rebuild and code items.</li>
<li>Work through differences with the insurer’s appraiser and, if necessary, present to the umpire.</li>
</ol>
<p>If your fire also involved water damage from firefighting, that belongs in the same scope. For terms such as “like kind and quality” or “actual cash value,” see the <a href="/claims-dictionary/">claims dictionary</a>. Fox Valley owners can visit our <a href="/local-appraisers/oshkosh/">Oshkosh</a> and <a href="/local-appraisers/appleton/">Appleton</a> pages, or <a href="/contact-us/">contact us</a> directly.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>The insurer wants to clean my walls, but the smell won’t go away. Can appraisal help?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, this is a common amount-of-loss dispute. Documentation and, where helpful, testing can support replacement when cleaning hasn’t worked.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal cover my personal property after a fire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies allow contents to be appraised along with the building. The panel values items according to the policy’s replacement cost or actual cash value terms.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I start repairs before the appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Protect the property from further damage, but avoid tearing out evidence before it is documented. Talk with your appraiser about what can wait.</p></div></details></div>
